# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Glossary batch 9 — terms from the 'Scaling light to the limiting factor' paper.
Plain-English, ASCII units (umol/m2/s, CO2) to match the rest of the glossary."""

GLOSSARY_ADD = [
    {"term": "Limiting factor (Liebig's law of the minimum)", "defn": "The limiting factor is the one input with the smallest supply compared with the quantity that the plant must have. Only this input sets the growth rate: more of this input increases the growth, and more of a different input gives no change. In a grow room, the weakest support system sets the limit of the yield (usually dehumidification, cooling or CO2), and not the brightest light that the fixtures can make.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Sensible heat load", "defn": "The sensible heat load is the dry heat that a grow room must remove, and it comes almost fully from the lights. Nearly all the power of the fixtures becomes heat, and this heat increases the air temperature and is the task of the air conditioner. As an estimate, the watts of light that go into the room are equal to the watts of dry heat that you must remove.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Latent heat load", "defn": "The latent heat load is the heat in water vapor, and the dehumidifier removes it when the vapor condenses to liquid. Each liter of water that the canopy transpires becomes latent load, and thus a bright room must have a large capacity of dehumidification. The latent load is different from the sensible heat load (dry heat), and at a high light intensity it is frequently the larger of the two.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Dehumidification load", "defn": "The dehumidification load is the quantity of water that a room must remove from the air each day to keep its humidity target. The transpiration of the canopy sets the load, and at a high light intensity the load is frequently the limit of the yield. The unit is liters or US pints each day (1 liter is approximately 2.1 pints), and a dense flowering canopy can transpire some liters for each square meter each day.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Mass flow (nutrient uptake)", "defn": "Mass flow is the primary movement of nutrients in solution to the root surface, with the water that the plant uses when it transpires. Faster transpiration moves more water and more nutrients, and thus the EC of the feed and the light intensity must increase together. If the feed is not sufficient for a bright canopy, the plants have less color.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Transpiration stream", "defn": "The transpiration stream is the continuous flow of water from the roots to the leaves, which the plant releases as vapor through the stomata. The flow moves the nutrients in solution up in the plant, and calcium moves almost fully in this flow. Thus weak transpiration shows as a calcium deficiency when the tank is full.", "tags": ["reference"]},
    {"term": "Boundary layer", "defn": "The boundary layer is the thin layer of air that does not move, on each leaf surface. CO2 that goes in, and water vapor and heat that go out, must diffuse slowly across the layer. Thus the layer sets a limit on the rate of work of the leaf. Air that moves (approximately 0.3 to 1.0 m/s at the canopy) makes the layer thinner. In a dense canopy, a layer in air that does not move sets a limit of CO2 that no room sensor shows.", "tags": ["reference"]},
]
